  "body": "Follow-up to #120, from two questions: **is a fallback inside the vision chain tracked**, and **does the request say which vision model was used**.\n\n## The vision chain was already tracked\n\nVerified against the real router and in the dashboard. A request whose vision model fails and whose *vision fallback* answers records:\n\n```\nroute_chain          chatgpt_oauth/gpt-5.6-luna,nous_portal/stepfun/step-3.7-flash:free,nvidia_nim/...\nroute_attempt        1\nroute_primary_model  chatgpt_oauth/gpt-5.6-luna\nroute_diverted_from  nous_portal/tencent/hy3:free\nroute_diversion      vision\n```\n\nand renders as **both** a `vision` badge and a `fallback 1, after chatgpt_oauth/gpt-5.6-luna` badge, with the trace marking which hop served. The failover panel shows the vision model's own failover; the diverted panel shows what each diversion was ultimately served by. No change needed.\n\n## Two real gaps, now closed\n\n**The vision model was only implied.** It is the head of the chain on a diverted request — readable only if you already know how diversion works. The detail now names it: `gpt-5.6-luna — answered`, or `gpt-5.6-luna — failed, answered by <model>`.\n\n**An image with nowhere to go recorded nothing.** When a request carries an image and no model on the route is known to accept one, the policy logs `VISION ROUTE UNAVAILABLE` and sends it anyway. The plan recorded no diversion — nothing was replaced — so the case an operator most needs to see looked like an ordinary request. `RouteDiversion` gains `VISION_UNAVAILABLE`, recorded with `route_diverted_from` NULL because nothing was in fact replaced.\n\nThat makes `route_diversion` no longer mean \"a diversion happened\", so both the window and lifetime counters now key on `route_diverted_from` — the field that actually means the head was replaced — and the window stats gain `vision_unavailable` beside it. The safety net working and the safety net having nowhere to put the request are different facts and must not be summed.\n\nDashboard: a `no vision route` badge, a bordered note naming the fix (`MODEL_VISION`), an \"Image, no vision route\" figure, and the \"Vision model\" row.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **All four policy branches probed**: adapter leads; adapter's own fallback answers; no adapter but a sighted entry leads; nothing sighted anywhere.\n- **Browser** on a throwaway home with all five row shapes — badges, both panels, trace hops and the new rows correct, no console errors.\n- **Contrast measured composited over the real backgrounds** in four themes: badge 15.3–18.0:1, note 14.0–19.8:1. My first attempt used `--warn` for the text, which is **4.1:1 on the light theme** — under AA — so warn moved to the border and fill.\n- Full suite **3861 passed**, 6 pre-existing PowerShell installer failures, identical to unmodified `fork/main`.\n\nOne existing test seeded `route_diversion` without `route_diverted_from`, a shape the router never writes; it now seeds both and pins that an unavailable row is not counted as a diversion.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **Rows written before this version keep the old counting.** A historical row with `route_diversion` set always has `route_diverted_from` set too, so the corrected counters read them identically — no backfill needed.\n- `vision_unavailable` is a window figure only; it is not in the lifetime rollup, which would need a `request_totals` column.\n\nVersion 5.13.0 → **5.14.0** (MINOR).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti\n",
